#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr "Isang libreng kursong IBM sa quantum na impormasyon at computation"

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Simon's algorithm"
msgstr "Algoritmo ni Simon"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"{/* cspell:ignore operatorname */}\n"
"{/* cspell:ignore Leftrightarrow */}\n"
msgstr ""
"{/* cspell:ignore operatorname */}\n"
"{/* cspell:ignore Leftrightarrow */}\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Simon's algorithm is a quantum query algorithm for a problem known as *Simon's problem*.\n"
"This is a promise problem with a flavor similar to the Deutsch-Jozsa and Bernstein-Vazirani problems, but the specifics are different.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang algoritmo ni Simon ay isang quantum query algorithm para sa problemang kilala bilang *problema ni Simon*.\n"
"Ito ay isang promise problem na may katulad na diwa sa Deutsch-Jozsa at Bernstein-Vazirani na mga problema, ngunit magkaiba ang mga detalye.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Simon's algorithm is significant because it provides an *exponential* advantage of quantum over classical (including probabilistic) algorithms, and the technique it uses inspired Peter Shor's discovery of an efficient quantum algorithm for integer factorization.\n"
msgstr "Ang algoritmo ni Simon ay mahalaga dahil nagbibigay ito ng *exponential* na kalamangan ng quantum kumpara sa classical (kasama ang probabilistic) na mga algorithm, at ang teknikang ginamit nito ang nagbigay inspirasyon kay Peter Shor para matuklasan ang isang episyenteng quantum algorithm para sa integer factorization.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Simon's problem {#simons-problem}"
msgstr "Problema ni Simon {#simons-problem}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The input function for Simon's problem takes the form\n"
msgstr "Ang input na function para sa problema ni Simon ay may anyo\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma^m\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma^m\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for positive integers $n$ and $m$.\n"
"We could restrict our attention to the case $m = n$ in the interest of simplicity, but there's little to be gained in making this assumption — Simon's algorithm and its analysis are basically the same either way.\n"
msgstr ""
"para sa mga positibong integer na $n$ at $m$.\n"
"Maaari nating limitahan ang ating atensyon sa kaso na $m = n$ para sa kasimplihan, ngunit wala masyadong mapapala sa paggawa ng assumption na ito — ang algoritmo ni Simon at ang pagsusuri nito ay halos pareho sa alinmang paraan.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Figure title=\"Simon's problem\">\n"
"Input: a function $f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma^m$ \\\n"
"Promise: there exists a string $s\\in\\Sigma^n$ such that $[f(x) = f(y)] \\Leftrightarrow\n"
"[(x = y) \\vee (x \\oplus s = y)]$ for all $x,y\\in\\Sigma^n$ \\\n"
"Output: the string $s$\n"
"</Figure>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Figure title=\"Problema ni Simon\">\n"
"Input: isang function na $f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma^m$ \\\n"
"Promise: may isang string na $s\\in\\Sigma^n$ na kung saan $[f(x) = f(y)] \\Leftrightarrow\n"
"[(x = y) \\vee (x \\oplus s = y)]$ para sa lahat ng $x,y\\in\\Sigma^n$ \\\n"
"Output: ang string na $s$\n"
"</Figure>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We'll unpack the promise to better understand what it says momentarily, but first let's be clear that it requires that $f$ has a very special structure — so most functions won't satisfy this promise.\n"
"It's also fitting to acknowledge that this problem isn't intended to have practical importance.\n"
"Rather, it's a somewhat artificial problem tailor-made to be easy for quantum computers and hard for classical computers.\n"
msgstr ""
"Tatalakayin natin ang promise para mas maunawaan kung ano ang sinasabi nito sa sandali, ngunit una, malinaw na ito ay nangangailangan na ang $f$ ay may napaka-espesyal na istruktura — kaya karamihan sa mga function ay hindi matutugunan ang promise na ito.\n"
"Angkop din na kilalanin na ang problemang ito ay hindi inilaan para magkaroon ng praktikal na kahalagahan.\n"
"Sa halip, ito ay isang medyo artipisyal na problema na espesyal na ginawa para maging madali para sa mga quantum computer at mahirap para sa mga classical computer.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are two main cases: the first case is that $s$ is the all-zero string $0^n$, and the second case is that $s$ is not the all-zero string.\n"
msgstr "May dalawang pangunahing kaso: ang unang kaso ay ang $s$ ay ang all-zero string na $0^n$, at ang ikalawang kaso ay ang $s$ ay hindi ang all-zero string.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Case 1: $s=0^n$.\n"
"If $s$ is the all-zero string, then we can simplify the if and only if statement in the promise so that it reads $[f(x) = f(y)] \\Leftrightarrow [x = y]$.\n"
"This is equivalent to $f$ being a one-to-one function.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kaso 1: $s=0^n.$\n"
"Kung ang $s$ ay ang all-zero string, maaari nating gawing mas simple ang if and only if na pahayag sa promise para mabasa itong $[f(x) = f(y)] \\Leftrightarrow [x = y].$\n"
"Ito ay katumbas ng $f$ na isang one-to-one na function.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Case 2: $s\\neq 0^n$.\n"
"If $s$ is not the all-zero string, then the promise being satisfied for this string implies that $f$ is *two-to-one*, meaning that for every possible output string of $f$, there are exactly two input strings that cause $f$ to output that string. Moreover, these two input strings must take the form $w$ and $w \\oplus s$ for some string $w$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kaso 2: $s\\neq 0^n.$\n"
"Kung ang $s$ ay hindi ang all-zero string, ang promise na natutugunan para sa string na ito ay nagpapahiwatig na ang $f$ ay *two-to-one*, ibig sabihin na para sa bawat posibleng output string ng $f,$ may eksaktong dalawang input string na nagdudulot sa $f$ na mag-output ng string na iyon. Bukod dito, ang dalawang input string na ito ay dapat nasa anyo ng $w$ at $w \\oplus s$ para sa ilang string na $w.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "It's important to recognize that there can only be one string $s$ that works if the promise is met, so there's always a unique correct answer for functions that satisfy the promise.\n"
msgstr "Mahalaga na kilalanin na iisang string lamang na $s$ ang maaaring gumana kung ang promise ay natutugunan, kaya lagi may natatanging tamang sagot para sa mga function na natutugunan ang promise.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here's an example of a function taking the form $f:\\Sigma^3 \\rightarrow \\Sigma^5$ that satisfies the promise for the string $s = 011$.\n"
msgstr "Narito ang isang halimbawa ng isang function na nasa anyo ng $f:\\Sigma^3 \\rightarrow \\Sigma^5$ na natutugunan ang promise para sa string na $s = 011$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"f(000) & = 10011 \\\\\n"
"f(001) & = 00101 \\\\\n"
"f(010) & = 00101 \\\\\n"
"f(011) & = 10011 \\\\\n"
"f(100) & = 11010 \\\\\n"
"f(101) & = 00001 \\\\\n"
"f(110) & = 00001 \\\\\n"
"f(111) & = 11010\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"f(000) & = 10011 \\\\\n"
"f(001) & = 00101 \\\\\n"
"f(010) & = 00101 \\\\\n"
"f(011) & = 10011 \\\\\n"
"f(100) & = 11010 \\\\\n"
"f(101) & = 00001 \\\\\n"
"f(110) & = 00001 \\\\\n"
"f(111) & = 11010\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There are $8$ different input strings and $4$ different output strings, each of which occurs twice — so this is a two-to-one function.\n"
"Moreover, for any two different input strings that produce the same output string, we see that the bitwise XOR of these two input strings is equal to $011$, which is equivalent to saying that either one of them equals the other XORed with $s$.\n"
msgstr ""
"May $8$ iba't ibang input string at $4$ iba't ibang output string, bawat isa ay lumalabas nang dalawang beses — kaya ito ay isang two-to-one na function.\n"
"Bukod dito, para sa anumang dalawang magkaibang input string na gumagawa ng parehong output string, makikita natin na ang bitwise XOR ng dalawang input string na ito ay katumbas ng $011$, na katumbas ng pagsasabi na alinman sa isa sa kanila ay katumbas ng isa pang XORed sa $s$.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Notice that the only thing that matters about the actual output strings is whether they're the same or different for different choices of input strings.\n"
"For instance, in the example above, there are four strings $(10011$, $00101$, $00001$, and $11010)$ that appear as outputs of $f$. We could replace these four strings with different strings, so long as they're all distinct, and the correct solution $s = 011$ would not change.\n"
msgstr ""
"Pansinin na ang tanging bagay na mahalaga tungkol sa aktwal na output string ay kung pareho o magkaiba ang mga ito para sa iba't ibang pagpipilian ng input string.\n"
"Halimbawa, sa halimbawa sa itaas, may apat na string $(10011,$ $00101,$ $00001,$ at $11010)$ na lumalabas bilang mga output ng $f.$ Maaari nating palitan ang apat na string na ito ng iba't ibang string, basta't magkakaiba ang lahat, at ang tamang solusyon na $s = 011$ ay hindi magbabago.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Algorithm description {#algorithm-description}"
msgstr "Paglalarawan ng algorithm {#algorithm-description}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here's a quantum circuit diagram representing Simon's algorithm.\n"
msgstr "Narito ang isang quantum circuit diagram na kumakatawan sa algoritmo ni Simon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Simon's algorithm](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/Simon.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Algoritmo ni Simon](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/Simon.svg)\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To be clear, there are $n$ qubits on the top that are acted upon by Hadamard gates and $m$ qubits on the bottom that go directly into the query gate.\n"
"It looks very similar to the algorithms we've already discussed in the lesson, but this time there's no phase kickback; the bottom $m$ qubits all go into the query gate in the state $\\vert 0\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para maging malinaw, may $n$ na qubit sa itaas na inaaksyunan ng mga Hadamard gate at $m$ na qubit sa ibaba na direktang pumapasok sa query gate.\n"
"Mukhang katulad ito ng mga algorithm na tinalakay na natin sa aralin, ngunit sa pagkakataong ito ay walang phase kickback; ang lahat ng $m$ na qubit sa ibaba ay pumapasok sa query gate sa estado na $\\vert 0\\rangle$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To solve Simon's problem using this circuit will actually require several independent runs of it followed by a classical post-processing step, which will be described later after the behavior of the circuit is analyzed.\n"
msgstr "Para malutas ang problema ni Simon gamit ang circuit na ito ay talagang mangangailangan ng ilang independyenteng pagpapatakbo nito na sinusundan ng isang classical post-processing na hakbang, na ilalarawan mamaya pagkatapos masuri ang gawi ng circuit.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Analysis {#analysis}"
msgstr "Pagsusuri {#analysis}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The analysis of Simon's algorithm begins along similar lines to the Deutsch-Jozsa algorithm.\n"
"After the first layer of Hadamard gates is performed on the top $n$ qubits, the state becomes\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang pagsusuri ng algoritmo ni Simon ay nagsisimula sa katulad na paraan sa algoritmo ng Deutsch-Jozsa.\n"
"Pagkatapos isagawa ang unang layer ng mga Hadamard gate sa itaas na $n$ na qubit, ang estado ay nagiging\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^n}} \\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma^n} \\vert 0^m \\rangle \\vert x\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^n}} \\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma^n} \\vert 0^m \\rangle \\vert x\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "When the $U_f$ is performed, the output of the function $f$ is XORed onto the all-zero state of the bottom $m$ qubits, so the state becomes\n"
msgstr "Kapag isasagawa ang $U_f,$ ang output ng function na $f$ ay XORed sa all-zero na estado ng ibabang $m$ na qubit, kaya ang estado ay nagiging\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^n}} \\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma^n} \\vert f(x) \\rangle \\vert x\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^n}} \\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma^n} \\vert f(x) \\rangle \\vert x\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "When the second layer of Hadamard gates is performed, we obtain the following state by using the same formula for the action of a layer of Hadamard gates as before.\n"
msgstr "Kapag isasagawa ang pangalawang layer ng mga Hadamard gate, makukuha natin ang sumusunod na estado sa pamamagitan ng paggamit ng parehong formula para sa aksyon ng isang layer ng mga Hadamard gate tulad ng dati.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2^n} \\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma^n} \\sum_{y\\in\\Sigma^n} (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\vert f(x) \\rangle \\vert y\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2^n} \\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma^n} \\sum_{y\\in\\Sigma^n} (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\vert f(x) \\rangle \\vert y\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "At this point, the analysis diverges from the ones for the previous algorithms in this lesson.\n"
msgstr "Sa puntong ito, ang pagsusuri ay nag-iiba mula sa mga nauna sa araling ito.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We're interested in the probability for the measurements to result in each possible string $y\\in\\Sigma^n$.\n"
"Through the rules for analyzing measurements described in the *Multiple systems* lesson of the *Basics of quantum information* course, we find that the probability $p(y)$ to obtain the string $y$ is equal to\n"
msgstr ""
"Interesado tayo sa probabilidad na ang mga sukat ay magreresulta sa bawat posibleng string na $y\\in\\Sigma^n$.\n"
"Sa pamamagitan ng mga panuntunan para sa pagsusuri ng mga sukat na inilarawan sa aralin na *Multiple systems* ng kursong *Basics of quantum information*, nalaman natin na ang probabilidad na $p(y)$ para makuha ang string na $y$ ay katumbas ng\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"p(y) = \\left\\|\\frac{1}{2^n} \\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma^n} (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\vert f(x) \\rangle \\right\\|^2.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"p(y) = \\left\\|\\frac{1}{2^n} \\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma^n} (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\vert f(x) \\rangle \\right\\|^2.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To get a better handle on these probabilities, we'll need just a bit more notation and terminology.\n"
"First, the *range* of the function $f$ is the set containing all of its output strings.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para mas maunawaan ang mga probabilidad na ito, kailangan natin ng kaunting karagdagang notasyon at terminolohiya.\n"
"Una, ang *range* ng function na $f$ ay ang set na naglalaman ng lahat ng output string nito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\operatorname{range}(f) = \\{ f(x) : x\\in \\Sigma^n \\}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\operatorname{range}(f) = \\{ f(x) : x\\in \\Sigma^n \\}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Second, for each string $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$, we can express the set of all input strings that cause the function to evaluate to this output string $z$ as $f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$.\n"
msgstr "Pangalawa, para sa bawat string na $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$, maaari nating ipahayag ang set ng lahat ng input string na nagdudulot sa function na mag-evaluate sa output string na $z$ bilang $f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"f^{-1}(\\{z\\}) = \\{ x\\in\\Sigma^n : f(x) = z \\}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"f^{-1}(\\{z\\}) = \\{ x\\in\\Sigma^n : f(x) = z \\}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The set $f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$ is known as the *preimage* of $\\{z\\}$ under $f$.\n"
"We can define the preimage under $f$ of any set in place of $\\{z\\}$ in an analogous way — it's the set of all elements that $f$ maps to that set.\n"
"(This notation should not be confused with the *inverse* of the function $f$, which may not exist.\n"
"The fact that the argument on the left-hand side is the set $\\{z\\}$ rather than the element $z$ is the clue that allows us to avoid this confusion.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang set na $f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$ ay kilala bilang ang *preimage* ng $\\{z\\}$ sa ilalim ng $f$.\n"
"Maaari nating tukuyin ang preimage sa ilalim ng $f$ ng anumang set sa lugar ng $\\{z\\}$ sa katulad na paraan — ito ang set ng lahat ng elemento na iminamapa ng $f$ sa set na iyon.\n"
"(Ang notasyong ito ay hindi dapat ipagkamali sa *inverse* ng function na $f$, na maaaring hindi umiiral.\n"
"Ang katotohanan na ang argumento sa kaliwang bahagi ay ang set na $\\{z\\}$ sa halip na ang elemento na $z$ ang palatandaan na nagpapahintulot sa atin na maiwasan ang pagkalito na ito.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Using this notation, we can split up the sum in our expression for the probabilities above\n"
"to obtain\n"
msgstr ""
"Gamit ang notasyong ito, maaari nating hatiin ang sum sa ating expression para sa mga probabilidad sa itaas\n"
"para makuha\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"p(y) =\n"
"\\left\\|\n"
"\\frac{1}{2^n}\n"
"\\sum_{z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)}\n"
"\\Biggl(\\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})}  (-1)^{x\\cdot y}\\Biggr)\n"
"\\vert z \\rangle\n"
"\\right\\|^2.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"p(y) =\n"
"\\left\\|\n"
"\\frac{1}{2^n}\n"
"\\sum_{z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)}\n"
"\\Biggl(\\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})}  (-1)^{x\\cdot y}\\Biggr)\n"
"\\vert z \\rangle\n"
"\\right\\|^2.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Every string $x\\in\\Sigma^n$ is represented exactly once by the two summations — we're basically just putting these strings into separate buckets depending on which output string $z = f(x)$ they produce when we evaluate the function $f$, and then summing separately over all the buckets.\n"
msgstr "Ang bawat string na $x\\in\\Sigma^n$ ay kinakatawan nang eksaktong isang beses ng dalawang summation — sa esensya ay inilalagay lamang natin ang mga string na ito sa magkakahiwalay na mga bucket depende kung aling output string na $z = f(x)$ ang magagawa nila kapag sinuri natin ang function na $f$, at pagkatapos ay magbibigay ng sum nang hiwalay sa lahat ng bucket.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can now evaluate the Euclidean norm squared to obtain\n"
msgstr "Maaari na nating suriin ang Euclidean norm squared para makuha\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"p(y) = \\frac{1}{2^{2n}}\n"
"\\sum_{z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)}\n"
"\\left\\vert \\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})}  (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\right\\vert^2.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"p(y) = \\frac{1}{2^{2n}}\n"
"\\sum_{z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)}\n"
"\\left\\vert \\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})}  (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\right\\vert^2.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To simplify these probabilities further, let's take a look at the value\n"
msgstr "Para mas gawing simple ang mga probabilidad na ito, tingnan natin ang halaga\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\left\\vert \\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})}  (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\right\\vert^2\n"
"\\tag{1}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\left\\vert \\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})}  (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\right\\vert^2\n"
"\\tag{1}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "for an arbitrary selection of $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$.\n"
msgstr "para sa isang arbitrary na pagpili ng $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If it happens to be the case that $s = 0^n$, then $f$ is a one-to-one function and there's always just a single element $x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$, for every $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$.\n"
"The value of the expression $(1)$ is $1$ in this case.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kung mangyari na ang $s = 0^n$, kung gayon ang $f$ ay isang one-to-one na function at lagi lamang may iisang elemento na $x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$, para sa bawat $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$.\n"
"Ang halaga ng expression na $(1)$ ay $1$ sa kasong ito.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If, on the other hand, $s\\neq 0^n$, then there are exactly two strings in the set $f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$.\n"
"To be precise, if we choose $w\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$ to be any one of these two strings, then the other string must be\n"
"$w \\oplus s$ by the promise in Simon's problem.\n"
"Using this observation we can simplify $(1)$ as follows.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kung, sa kabilang banda, ang $s\\neq 0^n,$ kung gayon may eksaktong dalawang string sa set na $f^{-1}(\\{z\\}).$\n"
"Para maging tumpak, kung pipiliin natin ang $w\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})$ na maging alinman sa dalawang string na ito, kung gayon ang kabilang string ay dapat\n"
"$w \\oplus s$ sa pamamagitan ng promise sa problema ni Simon.\n"
"Gamit ang obserbasyon na ito maaari nating gawing simple ang $(1)$ tulad ng sumusunod.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\left\\vert \\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})}  (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\right\\vert^2\n"
"& = \\Bigl\\vert (-1)^{w\\cdot y} + (-1)^{(w\\oplus s)\\cdot y} \\Bigr\\vert^2 \\\\\n"
"& = \\Bigl\\vert (-1)^{w\\cdot y} \\Bigl(1 + (-1)^{s\\cdot y}\\Bigr) \\Bigr\\vert^2 \\\\\n"
"& = \\Bigl\\vert 1 + (-1)^{y\\cdot s} \\Bigr\\vert^2 \\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{cases}\n"
"4 & y \\cdot s = 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & y \\cdot s = 1\n"
"\\end{cases}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\left\\vert \\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})}  (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\right\\vert^2\n"
"& = \\Bigl\\vert (-1)^{w\\cdot y} + (-1)^{(w\\oplus s)\\cdot y} \\Bigr\\vert^2 \\\\\n"
"& = \\Bigl\\vert (-1)^{w\\cdot y} \\Bigl(1 + (-1)^{s\\cdot y}\\Bigr) \\Bigr\\vert^2 \\\\\n"
"& = \\Bigl\\vert 1 + (-1)^{y\\cdot s} \\Bigr\\vert^2 \\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{cases}\n"
"4 & y \\cdot s = 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & y \\cdot s = 1\n"
"\\end{cases}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "So, it turns out that the value $(1)$ is independent of the specific choice of $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$ in both cases.\n"
msgstr "Kaya, lumalabas na ang halaga na $(1)$ ay independyente sa partikular na pagpili ng $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$ sa magkabilang kaso.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can now finish off the analysis by looking at the same two cases as before separately.\n"
msgstr "Maaari na nating tapusin ang pagsusuri sa pamamagitan ng pagtingin sa parehong dalawang kaso tulad ng dati nang hiwalay.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Case 1: $s = 0^n$. In this case the function $f$ is one-to-one, so there are $2^n$ strings $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)$, and we obtain\n"
msgstr "Kaso 1: $s = 0^n.$ Sa kasong ito ang function na $f$ ay one-to-one, kaya may $2^n$ na string na $z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f),$ at makukuha natin\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  p(y) = \\frac{1}{2^{2n}} \\cdot 2^n = \\frac{1}{2^n}.\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  p(y) = \\frac{1}{2^{2n}} \\cdot 2^n = \\frac{1}{2^n}.\n"
"  $$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  In words, the measurements result in a string $y\\in\\Sigma^n$ chosen uniformly at random.\n"
msgstr "  Sa madaling salita, ang mga sukat ay nagreresulta sa isang string na $y\\in\\Sigma^n$ na pinili nang uniformly at random.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Case 2: $s \\neq 0^n$.\n"
"In this case $f$ is two-to-one, so there are $2^{n-1}$ elements in $\\operatorname{range}(f)$.\n"
"Using the formula from above we conclude that the probability to measure each $y\\in\\Sigma^n$ is\n"
msgstr ""
"Kaso 2: $s \\neq 0^n.$\n"
"Sa kasong ito ang $f$ ay two-to-one, kaya may $2^{n-1}$ na elemento sa $\\operatorname{range}(f).$\n"
"Gamit ang formula mula sa itaas, napagpasyahan natin na ang probabilidad na sukatin ang bawat $y\\in\\Sigma^n$ ay\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  p(y)\n"
"  = \\frac{1}{2^{2n}} \\sum_{z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)}\n"
"  \\Biggl\\vert \\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})} (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\Biggr\\vert^2\n"
"  =\n"
"  \\begin{cases}\n"
"  \\frac{1}{2^{n-1}} & y \\cdot s = 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"  0 & y \\cdot s = 1\n"
"  \\end{cases}\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  p(y)\n"
"  = \\frac{1}{2^{2n}} \\sum_{z\\in\\operatorname{range}(f)}\n"
"  \\Biggl\\vert \\sum_{x\\in f^{-1}(\\{z\\})} (-1)^{x\\cdot y} \\Biggr\\vert^2\n"
"  =\n"
"  \\begin{cases}\n"
"  \\frac{1}{2^{n-1}} & y \\cdot s = 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"  0 & y \\cdot s = 1\n"
"  \\end{cases}\n"
"  $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  In words, we obtain a string chosen uniformly at random from the set $\\{y\\in\\Sigma^n : y \\cdot s = 0\\}$, which\n"
"  contains $2^{n-1}$ strings. (Because $s\\neq 0^n$, exactly half of the binary strings of length $n$ have binary dot\n"
"  product $1$ with $s$ and the other have binary dot product $0$ with $s$, as we already observed in the analysis of\n"
"  the Deutsch-Jozsa algorithm for the Bernstein-Vazirani problem.)\n"
msgstr ""
"  Sa madaling salita, nakukuha natin ang isang string na pinili nang uniformly at random mula sa set na $\\{y\\in\\Sigma^n : y \\cdot s = 0\\}$, na\n"
"  naglalaman ng $2^{n-1}$ na string. (Dahil ang $s\\neq 0^n$, eksaktong kalahati ng mga binary string na may haba $n$ ay may binary dot\n"
"  product na $1$ sa $s$ at ang iba pa ay may binary dot product na $0$ sa $s$, tulad ng naobserbahan na natin sa pagsusuri ng\n"
"  algoritmo ng Deutsch-Jozsa para sa problema ng Bernstein-Vazirani.)\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Classical post-processing {#classical-post-processing}"
msgstr "Classical post-processing {#classical-post-processing}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We now know what the probabilities are for the possible measurement outcomes when we run the quantum circuit for Simon's algorithm.\n"
"Is this enough information to determine $s$?\n"
msgstr ""
"Alam na natin kung ano ang mga probabilidad para sa mga posibleng resulta ng sukat kapag pinatakbo natin ang quantum circuit para sa algoritmo ni Simon.\n"
"Sapat na ba ang impormasyong ito para matukoy ang $s$?\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The answer is yes, provided that we're willing to repeat the process several times and accept that it could fail with some probability, which we can make very small by running the circuit enough times.\n"
"The essential idea is that each execution of the circuit provides us with statistical evidence concerning $s$, and we can use that evidence to find $s$ with very high probability if we run the circuit sufficiently many times.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang sagot ay oo, basta handa tayong ulitin ang proseso nang ilang beses at tanggapin na maaaring mabigo ito sa ilang probabilidad, na maaari nating gawing napakaliit sa pamamagitan ng pagpapatakbo ng circuit nang sapat na beses.\n"
"Ang pangunahing ideya ay ang bawat pagpapatupad ng circuit ay nagbibigay sa atin ng estadistikal na katibayan tungkol sa $s$, at maaari nating gamitin ang katibayan na iyon para mahanap ang $s$ na may napakataas na probabilidad kung pinatakbo natin ang circuit nang sapat na maraming beses.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's suppose that we run the circuit independently $k$ times, for $k = n + 10$.\n"
"There's nothing special about this particular number of iterations — we could take $k$ to be larger (or smaller) depending on the probability of failure we're willing to tolerate, as we will see.\n"
"Choosing $k = n + 10$ will ensure that we have greater than a $99.9$% chance of recovering $s$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ipagpalagay natin na pinatakbo natin ang circuit nang nakapag-iisang $k$ beses, para sa $k = n + 10$.\n"
"Walang espesyal tungkol sa partikular na bilang ng pag-uulit na ito — maaari tayong kumuha ng mas malaki (o mas maliit) na $k$ depende sa probabilidad ng pagkabigo na handa tayong tiisin, tulad ng makikita natin.\n"
"Ang pagpili ng $k = n + 10$ ay titiyak na mayroon tayong mahigit $99.9$% na pagkakataon ng pagbawi ng $s$.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"By running the circuit $k$ times, we obtain strings $y^1,...,y^{k} \\in \\Sigma^n$.\n"
"To be clear, the superscripts here are part of the names of these strings, not exponents or indexes to their bits, so we have\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa pamamagitan ng pagpapatakbo ng circuit nang $k$ beses, makukuha natin ang mga string na $y^1,...,y^{k} \\in \\Sigma^n$.\n"
"Para maging malinaw, ang mga superscript dito ay bahagi ng mga pangalan ng mga string na ito, hindi mga exponent o mga index sa kanilang mga bit, kaya mayroon tayo\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"y^1 & = y^1_{n-1} \\cdots y^1_{0}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"y^2 & = y^2_{n-1} \\cdots y^2_{0}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"& \\;\\; \\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"y^{k} & = y^{k}_{n-1} \\cdots y^{k}_{0}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"y^1 & = y^1_{n-1} \\cdots y^1_{0}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"y^2 & = y^2_{n-1} \\cdots y^2_{0}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"& \\;\\; \\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"y^{k} & = y^{k}_{n-1} \\cdots y^{k}_{0}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We now form a matrix $M$ having $k$ rows and $n$ columns by taking the bits of these strings as binary-valued entries.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon ay bumubuo tayo ng matris na $M$ na may $k$ na row at $n$ na column sa pamamagitan ng pagkuha ng mga bit ng mga string na ito bilang mga binary-valued na entry.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"M = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
" y^1_{n-1} & \\cdots & y^1_{0}\\\\[1mm]\n"
" y^2_{n-1} & \\cdots & y^2_{0}\\\\[1mm]\n"
" \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\[1mm]\n"
" y^{k}_{n-1} & \\cdots & y^{k}_{0}\n"
" \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"M = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
" y^1_{n-1} & \\cdots & y^1_{0}\\\\[1mm]\n"
" y^2_{n-1} & \\cdots & y^2_{0}\\\\[1mm]\n"
" \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\[1mm]\n"
" y^{k}_{n-1} & \\cdots & y^{k}_{0}\n"
" \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, we don't know what $s$ is at this point — our goal is to find this string.\n"
"But imagine for a moment that we do know the string $s$, and we form a column vector $v$ from the bits of the string $s = s_{n-1} \\cdots s_0$ as follows.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ngayon, hindi natin alam kung ano ang $s$ sa puntong ito — ang ating layunin ay mahanap ang string na ito.\n"
"Ngunit isipin sandali na alam natin ang string na $s$, at bumubuo tayo ng column vector na $v$ mula sa mga bit ng string na $s = s_{n-1} \\cdots s_0$ tulad ng sumusunod.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"v = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"s_{n-1}\\\\\n"
"\\vdots\\\\\n"
"s_0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"v = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"s_{n-1}\\\\\n"
"\\vdots\\\\\n"
"s_0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "If we perform the matrix-vector multiplication $M v$ modulo $2$ — meaning that we perform the multiplication as usual and then take the remainder of the entries of the result after dividing by $2$ — we obtain the all-zero vector.\n"
msgstr "Kung isasagawa natin ang matrix-vector multiplication na $M v$ modulo $2$ — ibig sabihin, ginagawa natin ang multiplication tulad ng dati at pagkatapos ay kinukuha ang natitirang bahagi ng mga entry ng resulta pagkatapos hatiin sa $2$ — makukuha natin ang all-zero vector.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"M v = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"y^1 \\cdot s\\\\\n"
"y^2 \\cdot s\\\\\n"
"\\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"y^{k} \\cdot s\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0\\\\\n"
"0\\\\\n"
"\\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"M v = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"y^1 \\cdot s\\\\\n"
"y^2 \\cdot s\\\\\n"
"\\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"y^{k} \\cdot s\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0\\\\\n"
"0\\\\\n"
"\\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"That is, treated as a column vector $v$ as just described, the string $s$ will always be an element of the *null space* of the matrix $M$, provided that we do the arithmetic modulo $2$.\n"
"This is true in both the case that $s = 0^n$ and $s\\neq 0^n$.\n"
"To be more precise, the all-zero vector is always in the null space of $M$, and it's joined by the vector whose entries are the bits of $s$ in case $s\\neq 0^n$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ibig sabihin, na tinatrato bilang isang column vector na $v$ tulad ng inilarawan lamang, ang string na $s$ ay laging magiging isang elemento ng *null space* ng matris na $M$, basta ginagawa natin ang aritmetika modulo $2$.\n"
"Ito ay totoo sa parehong kaso na $s = 0^n$ at $s\\neq 0^n$.\n"
"Para maging mas tumpak, ang all-zero vector ay laging nasa null space ng $M$, at sinasamahan ito ng vector na ang mga entry ay ang mga bit ng $s$ sa kaso na ang $s\\neq 0^n$.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The question remaining is whether there will be any other vectors in the null space of $M$ besides the ones corresponding to $0^n$ and $s$.\n"
"The answer is that this becomes increasingly unlikely as $k$ increases — and if we choose $k = n + 10$, the null space of $M$ will contain no other vectors in addition to those corresponding to $0^n$ and $s$ with greater than a $99.9$% chance.\n"
"More generally, if we replace $k = n + 10$ with $k = n + r$ for an arbitrary choice of a positive integer $r$, the probability that the vectors corresponding to $0^n$ and $s$ are alone in the null space of $M$ is at least $1 - 2^{-r}$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang natitirang tanong ay kung magkakaroon pa ng ibang mga vector sa null space ng $M$ bukod sa mga katumbas ng $0^n$ at $s$.\n"
"Ang sagot ay ito ay nagiging lalong hindi malamang habang tumaas ang $k$ — at kung pipiliin natin ang $k = n + 10$, ang null space ng $M$ ay hindi maglalaman ng ibang mga vector bukod pa sa mga katumbas ng $0^n$ at $s$ na may mahigit $99.9$% na pagkakataon.\n"
"Sa pangkalahatan, kung papalitan natin ang $k = n + 10$ ng $k = n + r$ para sa isang arbitrary na pagpili ng positibong integer na $r$, ang probabilidad na ang mga vector na katumbas ng $0^n$ at $s$ ay mag-isa sa null space ng $M$ ay hindi bababa sa $1 - 2^{-r}$.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Using linear algebra, it is possible to efficiently calculate a description of the null space of $M$ modulo $2$.\n"
"Specifically, it can be done using *Gaussian elimination*, which works the same way when arithmetic is done modulo $2$ as it does with real or complex numbers.\n"
"So long as the vectors corresponding to $0^n$ and $s$ are alone in the null space of $M$, which happens with high probability, we can deduce $s$ from the results of this computation.\n"
msgstr ""
"Gamit ang linear algebra, posibleng mahusay na kalkulahin ang isang paglalarawan ng null space ng $M$ modulo $2$.\n"
"Partikular, maaari itong gawin gamit ang *Gaussian elimination*, na gumagana sa parehong paraan kapag ang aritmetika ay ginagawa modulo $2$ tulad ng sa mga tunay o kumplikadong numero.\n"
"Basta ang mga vector na katumbas ng $0^n$ at $s$ ay mag-isa sa null space ng $M$, na nangyayari nang may mataas na probabilidad, maaari nating mahinuha ang $s$ mula sa mga resulta ng computation na ito.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Classical difficulty {#classical-difficulty}"
msgstr "Kahirapan ng classical {#classical-difficulty}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"How many queries does a *classical* query algorithm need to solve Simon's problem?\n"
"The answer is: a lot, in general.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ilang query ang kailangan ng isang *classical* query algorithm para malutas ang problema ni Simon?\n"
"Ang sagot ay: marami, sa pangkalahatan.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There are different precise statements that can be made about the classical difficulty of this problem, and here's just one of them.\n"
"If we have any probabilistic query algorithm, and that algorithm makes fewer than $2^{n/2 - 1} - 1$ queries, which is a number of queries that's *exponential* in $n$, then that algorithm will fail to solve Simon's problem with probability at least $1/2$.\n"
msgstr ""
"May iba't ibang tumpak na pahayag na maaaring gawin tungkol sa kahirapan ng classical ng problemang ito, at narito ang isa sa kanila.\n"
"Kung mayroon tayong anumang probabilistic query algorithm, at ang algorithm na iyon ay gumagawa ng mas kaunting $2^{n/2 - 1} - 1$ na query, na isang bilang ng query na *exponential* sa $n$, kung gayon ang algorithm na iyon ay mabibigo sa paglutas ng problema ni Simon na may probabilidad na hindi bababa sa $1/2$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sometimes, proving impossibility results like this can be very challenging, but this one isn't too difficult to prove through an elementary probabilistic analysis.\n"
"Here, however, we'll only briefly examine the basic intuition behind it.\n"
msgstr ""
"Minsan, ang pagpapatunay ng mga imposibilidad na resulta tulad nito ay maaaring napakahirap, ngunit ang ito ay hindi masyadong mahirap patunayan sa pamamagitan ng isang elementaryang probabilistic na pagsusuri.\n"
"Dito, gayunpaman, ay maikling susuriin lamang natin ang pangunahing intuisyon sa likod nito.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We're trying to find the hidden string $s$, but so long as we don't query the function on two strings having the same output value, we'll get very limited information about $s$.\n"
"Intuitively speaking, all we'll learn is that the hidden string $s$ is *not* the exclusive-OR of any two distinct strings we've queried.\n"
"And if we query fewer than $2^{n/2 - 1} - 1$ strings, then there will still be a lot of choices for $s$ that we haven't ruled out because there aren't enough pairs of strings to do this.\n"
"This isn't a formal proof, it's just the basic idea.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sinusubukan nating mahanap ang nakatagong string na $s$, ngunit basta hindi tayo nag-query ng function sa dalawang string na may parehong output value, makakakuha tayo ng limitadong impormasyon tungkol sa $s$.\n"
"Sa intuwisyon, ang matututunan lamang natin ay ang nakatagong string na $s$ ay *hindi* ang exclusive-OR ng anumang dalawang magkaibang string na na-query natin.\n"
"At kung mag-query tayo ng mas kaunting $2^{n/2 - 1} - 1$ na string, magkakaroon pa rin ng maraming pagpipilian para sa $s$ na hindi pa natin na-rule out dahil hindi sapat ang mga pares ng string para gawin ito.\n"
"Hindi ito isang pormal na patunay, ito lamang ang pangunahing ideya.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"So, in summary, Simon's algorithm provides us with a striking advantage of quantum over classical algorithms within the query model.\n"
"In particular, Simon's algorithm solves Simon's problem with a number of queries that's *linear* in the number of input bits $n$ of our function, whereas any classical algorithm, even if it's probabilistic, needs to make a number of queries that's *exponential* in $n$ in order to solve Simon's problem with a reasonable probability of success.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kaya, sa buod, ang algoritmo ni Simon ay nagbibigay sa atin ng kapansin-pansing kalamangan ng quantum kumpara sa mga classical algorithm sa loob ng query model.\n"
"Sa partikular, ang algoritmo ni Simon ay naglulutas ng problema ni Simon gamit ang isang bilang ng query na *linear* sa bilang ng input bits na $n$ ng ating function, samantalang ang anumang classical algorithm, kahit probabilistic, ay kailangang gumawa ng isang bilang ng query na *exponential* sa $n$ para malutas ang problema ni Simon na may makatwirang probabilidad ng tagumpay.\n"
